rotection or recognition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vassal-and-tributary-systems"><span class="enc-name">Vassal and tributary systems</span><span class="enc-type">Historical factor</span><span class="enc-snip">Vassal and tributary systems describe the recurring pattern by which empires ruled indirectly through subordinate rulers who kept local authority in exchange for…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vastergotland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Västergötland</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Västergötland is a historical province in south-western Sweden, between lakes Vänern and Vättern and the Kattegat coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vastgotalagen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vastgotalagen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Västgötalagen</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Västgötalagen, or Västgöta Law, is the provincial law of Västergötland in Sweden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasto"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vasto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vasto</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasto is a coastal town in the province of Chieti in Abruzzo, Italy, on the Adriatic.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vastryoshan"><span class="enc-name">Vastryoshan</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The vastryoshan were the commoners in the social hierarchy of the Sasanian Empire, comprising both peasant cultivators and artisans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vastupala"><span class="enc-name">Vastupala</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vastupala was a Jain minister of the Vaghela rulers of Gujarat, serving Viradhavala at Dholka with his younger brother Tejapala from about 1220.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vasudeva.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vasudeva</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasudeva is a Hindu deity, originally a hero of the Vrishni clan of Mathura who was worshipped as a god and later merged with Krishna and identified with Vishnu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vasudeva I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasudeva I was the last great emperor of the Kushan Empire, whose inscriptions date roughly from 190 to 230 CE and are concentrated around Mathura.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vasudeva-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vasudeva II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasudeva II was one of the last Kushan kings, ruling in the later 3rd century CE over a much reduced realm around the Indus and the Punjab.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-kanva"><span class="enc-name">Vasudeva Kanva</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasudeva Kanva was a Brahmin minister who founded the Kanva dynasty of Magadha in northern India around 73 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasyl-chuchupak"><span class="enc-name">Vasyl Chuchupak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasyl Chuchupak was a Ukrainian insurgent leader who in 1919 organised a peasant self-defence force around the Motronynskyi Monastery in the Kholodny Yar forest of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasylko-rostyslavych"><span class="enc-name">Vasylko Rostyslavych</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vasylko Rostyslavych was prince of Terebovl in Galicia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vata-pagan-uprising"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vata-pagan-uprising.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vata pagan uprising</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1046</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vata pagan uprising was a revolt in Hungary in 1046, led by a nobleman named Vata, against Christianity and the rule of King Peter Orseolo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vathy-samos"><span class="enc-name">Vathy</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vathy is the main town and port of the Greek island of Samos, on a deep bay of its north-eastern coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-library.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vatican Library</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vatican Library is the library of the Holy See in Vatican City, formally established in 1475 by Pope Sixtus IV, building on the collection of Nicholas V.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-museums"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-museums.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vatican Museums</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vatican Museums are the art and archaeological museums of the popes in Vatican City, begun in 1506 when Pope Julius II displayed the newly found statue of Laocoön.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-radio"><span class="enc-name">Vatican Radio</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Vatican Radio is the broadcasting service of the Holy See, set up by Guglielmo Marconi and inaugurated by Pope Pius XI on 12 February 1931.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatsagulma"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vatsagulma.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vatsagulma</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vatsagulma was the capital of the western branch of the Vakataka dynasty, in the Deccan of India, identified with modern Washim in Maharashtra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja"><span class="enc-name">Vatsaraja</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vatsaraja was a king of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty in the late 8th century who made it a major power in northern India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vaud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vaud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vaud</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vaud is a French-speaking canton of western Switzerland, stretching from Lake Geneva to the Jura with Lausanne as its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vazimba"><span class="enc-name">Vazimba</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vazimba are, in Malagasy tradition, the earliest inhabitants of the central highlands of Madagascar, displaced or absorbed by the ancestors of the Merina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vechta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vechta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vechta</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vechta is a town in Lower Saxony, Germany, in the Oldenburg Münsterland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vechte"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vechte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vechte</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vechte is a river about 180 kilometres long that rises in Westphalia, Germany, flows through the former County of Bentheim past Nordhorn and enters the Netherlands…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedanta"><span class="enc-name">Vedanta</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Vedanta is a group of schools of Hindu philosophy based on the Upanishads, the Brahma Sutras and the Bhagavad Gita, concerned with the nature of the ultimate reality…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vedas</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vedas are the oldest scriptures of Hinduism, composed in Vedic Sanskrit in north-west India from about 1500 BCE and transmitted orally for centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedeno"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vedeno.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vedeno</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vedeno is a village in the mountains of south-eastern Chechnya, Russia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-i"><span class="enc-name">Veera Ballala I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Veera Ballala I was a ruler of the Hoysala dynasty in southern Karnataka at the start of the 12th century, the eldest son of the prince Ereyanga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veera Ballala II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Veera Ballala II was king of the Hoysala Empire in southern India from 1173 to 1220.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-iii"><span class="enc-name">Veera Ballala III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Veera Ballala III was the last major king of the Hoysala Empire, ruling from 1292 to 1343.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veere"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veere.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veere</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Veere is a small town on the former island of Walcheren in the Dutch province of Zeeland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vef"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vef.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">VEF</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">VEF (Valsts Elektrotehniskā Fabrika, State Electrotechnical Factory) was a state-owned electrical manufacturer in Riga, established in 1919.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vega-of-granada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vega-of-granada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vega of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vega of Granada is the fertile plain around the city of Granada in Andalusia, Spain, watered by the Genil river and its tributaries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vega-real"><span class="enc-name">Vega Real</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vega Real, 'royal plain', is a fertile valley in the north-central Dominican Republic, the eastern part of the Cibao valley drained by the Yuna River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vegesack"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vegesack.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vegesack</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vegesack is a district of Bremen on the Weser, about 20 kilometres downstream from the city centre.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veit-stoss"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veit-stoss.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veit Stoss</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Veit Stoss was a German sculptor and woodcarver of the late Gothic period, active mainly in Nuremberg and Kraków.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vel-dhiv-roundup"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vel-dhiv-roundup.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vel' d'Hiv Roundup</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vel' d'Hiv Roundup was a mass arrest of Jews in Paris on 16–17 July 1942, carried out by French police under the Vichy government at German request.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vela-jimenez"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vela-jimenez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vela Jiménez</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vela Jiménez was count of Álava in the late 9th century, one of the frontier lords of the Kingdom of Asturias.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/velama"><span class="enc-name">Velama</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Velamas are a Telugu-speaking landholding community of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana in southern India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/velay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/velay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Velay</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Velay is a historic region of the Massif Central in south-central France, around Le Puy-en-Velay, named after the Gallic Vellavi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veleda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veleda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veleda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Veleda was a seeress of the Bructeri in Germany in the 1st century CE, described by Tacitus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veliko Tarnovo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Veliko Tarnovo is a city in north-central Bulgaria on the Yantra river, built on hills including Tsarevets and Trapezitsa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veliky Novgorod</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Veliky Novgorod is one of the oldest cities of Russia, on the Volkhov river near Lake Ilmen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/veliky-ustyug"><span class="enc-name">Veliky Ustyug</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Veliky Ustyug is a town in Vologda Oblast in northern Russia, at the confluence of the Sukhona and Yug rivers, first mentioned in the chronicles in 1207.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Velleius Paterculus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Velleius Paterculus was a Roman soldier and historian who served under Tiberius in Germany and Pannonia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/velletri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/velletri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Velletri</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Velletri is a town in the Alban Hills south-east of Rome, Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vellore"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/vellore.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vellore</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vellore is a city in the north of Tamil Nadu, India, known for its sixteenth-century fort built under the Vijayanagara Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/velu-thampi-dalawa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/velu-thampi-dalawa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Velu Thampi Dalawa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Velu Thampi Dalawa was the chief minister, or dalawa, of the kingdom of Travancore from 1802 to 1809.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/veluvana"><span class="enc-name">Veluvana</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Veluvana, the Bamboo Grove, was a park outside Rajagriha, the capital of Magadha, which King Bimbisara gave to the Buddha and his order of monks.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/veluwe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/veluwe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Veluwe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Veluwe is a forested ridge of sandy hills in the Dutch province of Gelderland, between Arnhem, Apeldoorn and the IJssel.</span></a></li></ul>
